cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite the archived version below."
title: "Do machine-learning rankings find Fermi-LAT pulsars better than spectral curvature? A pre-registered temporal-holdout evaluation"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Kondekar
    given-names: Raj
version: 1.0.0
date-released: 2026-11-27
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/astrophile73/4FGL-Hierarchical-Classification"
# doi: 10.5281/zenodo.XXXXXXX   <- fill in with the DOI reserved on Zenodo before tagging v1.0.0
abstract: >-
  Pre-registered evaluation of pulsar-candidate rankings for unassociated Fermi-LAT sources on three
  disjoint temporal holdouts (3FGL to 4FGL-DR3, 4FGL-DR3 to DR4, 4FGL-DR4 to FL16Y; 102 later-identified
  pulsars). A random forest is at best marginally better than ranking by spectral-curvature significance
  alone (difference in AUC +0.017 to +0.040; no 95% interval excludes zero). In-distribution
  cross-validation (AUC 0.98) strongly overstates performance on later discoveries (0.78 to 0.83), and an
  exploratory millisecond-pulsar advantage was not confirmed in a pre-registered test. Includes a leakage
  audit showing that an earlier 98.84% balanced-accuracy claim was an artifact of SMOTE applied before
  cross-validation.
keywords:
  - gamma-ray astronomy
  - Fermi-LAT
  - pulsars
  - millisecond pulsars
  - unassociated sources
  - machine learning
  - pre-registration
  - temporal validation
